020,"team":"HOU","distance":0.8938,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"JAX","distance":0.8989,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Broncos went 6-10 in 2018 and were outscored 349 to 329. The offense ranked 21st in EPA per play and the defense 16th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. What set them apart was the 3rd highest I formation usage and the highest base defense usage in the league.","offense":"The Broncos offense was a little below average by EPA per play, and it was much better running the ball than passing it. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the 3rd highest I formation usage in the league. Case Keenum ran the passing game, with Emmanuel Sanders leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Broncos defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was takeaways per game, where it ranked 5th, and its weakest was pressure rate, where it ranked 22nd. Von Miller led the team with 14.5 sacks, and Adam Gotsis added 3 despite playing only 48% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the highest base defense usage in the league.","special":"The Broncos were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 80% of field goals, which was well below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 22% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 40% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Case Keenum played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,890 yards. Targets were spread around, with Emmanuel Sanders and Courtland Sutton leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by Phillip Lindsay and Royce Freeman. On defense, Von Miller led the team with 14.5 sacks and Justin Simmons picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Texans under Bobby Slowik and the 2019 Patriots under Josh McDaniels. Their defense looked most like the 2018 Rams and the 2021 Steelers.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the Broncos changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}